From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 20:27:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 113/144] feat(polecat): run preflight and self-review through
 known-red (gcp-oczc)

gc-cgxps finding #4: prose telling a polecat to "file a bead" does not
survive a fresh session, so the same base red was re-diagnosed by up to 7
agents and re-filed up to 4 times. gcp-4ngf vendored the known-red matcher
into assets/scripts; this wires the polecat side of mol-polecat-work to it
(Hunter's Q1 ruling, 2026-09-27).

preflight-tests is now overridden here. Every phase runs through gate_run,
which writes the `gate(<phase>): RUN` / PASS / FAIL segments known-red
splits on. A phase an open base-red bead already covers is not re-run on
base; kr_phase_covered fails closed, so a lookup that errors, a header that
does not parse, or a row count that disagrees with it never skips a phase.
A red is matched: all KNOWN (or FIXED-UPSTREAM) is noted on the work bead
and nothing is filed; NEW goes through `known-red file` and the witness
NOTICE names the bead it filed; a failed lookup files nothing and says so.
Without a resolvable known-red the step falls back to the base's hand-filed
bead.

self-review runs its checks and tests through the same gate_run into its
own log, and asks known-red about any red. The old "If pre-existing, file a
bead." is replaced by the attribution flow: KNOWN is not yours; each NEW id
is re-run alone on origin/<base> in a detached worktree (go/bats/pytest ids
narrowly, anything else by its phase command), and only ids red on base
are filed through known-red.

Phases are named after the formula var holding the command, and a
configured command's red is never filed under another name, because the
skip keys on the phase: gcp-98gh filed as phase=test would have skipped
go test on this rig.

Each step's functions and the code using them share one fenced block,
since an agent's shell state does not survive between Bash calls; the two
shared blocks are identical in both steps. The blocks avoid backslashes,
which a TOML basic string would turn into escapes.

gastown/tests/test_known_red_wiring.sh executes every block from the
shipped formula against a stub gc (formula list from a canned file, ledger
calls to tests/fixtures/known_red/fake_gc.py) and the real known-red.
It fails on c77cf7e5, where the blocks do not exist, and each of seven
formula mutations (no skip, no eval, no GC_PACK_DIR fallback, copy drift,
dropped exit/count/header checks) turns it red.

